+<p>
+The RDF (Turtle) format represents the same abstract resource content as XML and JSON,
+but uses RDF graph conventions that create additional syntax differences:
+</p>
+<ul>
+ <li>Repeating elements are represented as <a href="rdf.html#rdflist">RDF lists</a> in order to retain the order of their elements</li>
+ <li>FHIR choice elements, such as <code>value[x]</code>, are represented in XML and JSON by appending the selected type to the element or property name, but are represented in RDF as a <a href="rdf.html#propertyNames">polymorphic property</a> whose object asserts its type explicitly</li>
+ <li>Primitive elements are represented as anonymous nodes containing a <code>fhir:v</code> literal, so that extensions and RDF types can be represented consistently</li>
+ <li>FHIR RDF includes a few FHIR-specific terms, including <code>fhir:nodeRole</code>, <code>fhir:treeRoot</code>, and <code>fhir:v</code>, as described in the <a href="rdf.html#relationship">RDF relationship</a> guidance</li>
+</ul>
 
